course: dictionary
lesson: 1154
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Brilliant is one of them. To find the right meaning, read the words around it, which are its context.
explain: «The brilliant sunshine made the lake sparkle.» Here brilliant means very bright and shining, because the clues are the sunshine and the sparkling lake. «Ruby's brilliant idea solved the problem.» Here brilliant means very clever, because an idea can be clever but not shining.
explain: Brilliant also means really good or great, especially in everyday speech in Britain, Ireland and Australia. Jack says, «Oh brilliant, best day ever» about a late bus. The clues show he is joking, because a late bus is not good news.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Does «a brilliant idea» still make sense as «a very bright idea»? No, so the meaning must be very clever.
fact: Brilliant means very clever or talented, as in «a brilliant detective».
fact: Brilliant means very bright and shining, as in «brilliant sunshine».
fact: Brilliant means really good or great, as in «That's brilliant!» or «a brilliant story».
fact: Many British people praise by playing down, so «the girls did alright today» after a 5–0 cup final win means they were brilliant.
fact: In Liverpool, a big lad says «boss» to mean brilliant, so context and local speech both matter.
exercise: «The brilliant light from the lighthouse cut through the fog.» What does brilliant mean here? | *Very bright and shining | Very clever | Very dark | Really good, great
exercise: Ella's friend wins a prize, and Ella says «That's brilliant!» What does brilliant mean? | Very late | *Really good, great | Very bright | Very clever
exercise: «The brilliant detective solved the case in one hour.» What does brilliant mean? | Very old | Very wet | Very bright and shining | *Very clever or talented
test: «She had a brilliant idea for the science fair.» What does brilliant mean? | *Very clever | Very quiet | Very old | Very bright
test: After a 5–0 cup final win, a British fan says «the girls did alright today.» What does this suggest? | They were tired | They were wet | *They were brilliant | They were late
exam: «The painted wall was covered in brilliant colours.» Which meaning fits? | Very old | Very clever | *Very bright and shining | Really good, great
